# Locator Strategy Rules

## Purpose

These rules define how UI locators should be selected.

The goal is to keep selectors stable, readable, and aligned with Playwright best practices.

---

## Locator Priority

Prefer stable, user-facing locators.

Recommended priority:

1. `getByRole` with accessible name;
2. `getByLabel`;
3. `getByPlaceholder`;
4. `getByText` when stable and meaningful;
5. `getByTestId`;
6. CSS only when no semantic locator is available.

Avoid XPath, generated CSS classes, `nth-child`, and layout-dependent selectors unless explicitly justified.

---

## Test ID Strategy

Use the project-configured test id strategy consistently.

If Playwright is configured with:

```ts
testIdAttribute: "data-test"
```

then use:

```ts
page.getByTestId("login-submit");
```

Do not use:

```ts
page.locator('[data-test="login-submit"]');
```

The locator API should reflect the configured framework convention.

---

## Stable Locators

Good:

```ts
page.getByRole("button", { name: "Sign in" });
```

Good:

```ts
page.getByLabel("Email");
```

Good:

```ts
page.getByTestId("login-submit");
```

Bad:

```ts
page.locator(".css-1x2abc");
```

Bad:

```ts
page.locator("body > div:nth-child(2) > div > button");
```

Bad:

```ts
page.locator("//html/body/div[3]/div/button");
```

---

## Locator Ownership

Do not scatter raw selectors across specs.

Locators should normally live in:

- Page Objects;
- Component Objects;
- dedicated low-level UI helpers when truly generic.

Specs should express user intent, not selector mechanics.

Good:

```ts
await loginPage.login(user.email, user.password);
```

Avoid:

```ts
await page.locator('[data-test="email"]').fill(user.email);
await page.locator('[data-test="password"]').fill(user.password);
await page.locator('[data-test="login-submit"]').click();
```

---

## Fragile Locator Exception

Fragile locators are allowed only as a temporary last resort when:

- there is no semantic locator;
- there is no test id;
- the UI cannot be changed immediately;
- the reason is documented;
- the locator is isolated inside a Page Object or Component Object.

Do not place fragile locators directly in specs.

---

## Main Principle

Use locators that describe stable user-facing behavior or explicit test contracts.

Avoid locators that depend on layout, generated classes, or incidental DOM structure.